Finding: my AS2-eth0 had net.ipv4.conf.*.send_redirects=1. Combined with my current default route (0.0.0.0/0 via 10.0.2.1, i.e. back to you — I have no other upstream in this topology), any packet arriving on AS2-eth0 for an unknown destination like 10.0.0.99 hairpins out the same interface to a next-hop on the same subnet, which is exactly the condition that triggers ICMP Redirect. Hence the loop your customer's traceroute saw.

Fix applied (local, reversible): disabled send_redirects on all interfaces. Verified the kernel route cache no longer marks 10.0.0.99 with <redirect>. Packets will still loop at the IP layer until TTL expiry (because my default still points to you and 10.0.0.99 is RFC1918 / globally unreachable), but the misleading ICMP Redirect noise on traceroute should be gone.

Residual: traffic to truly unroutable destinations will still bounce between us until TTL=0. A cleaner fix would be a discard/null route for RFC1918 on one of us, but that's a policy decision I'll leave to admins. Let me know if you'd like me to install one on my side.
